    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my diesel mech exam in munich and i'm a bit stuck on turbochargers. can u explain how the exhaust gas actually turns the turbine and how that leads to more air getting into the engine? specifically how the pressure increases in the manifold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exhaust gases from the engine exit the cylinder, withning the exhaust manifold, and this high-velocity, high-temperature stream is forced through the nozzle of the turbine followed by the turbine wheel.\n\nAs the gases expand and lose their thermal energy, they spin the turbine blades at extremely high speeds, which acts like a pump for diesel engines by compressing intake air.\n\nThis spinning action drives the compressor on the opposite side of the turbine housing, pressurizing the incoming air and sending it through the intercooler before forcing it into the engine's intake manifold to cool the diesel fuel.",
      "source": "target",
